<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raqqa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raqqa is a city on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Known in Hellenistic and Roman times as Nikephorion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kallinikos">Kallinikos</a>, it was the residence of the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> from 796 to 808, and in the 10th and 11th centuries it was held in turn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> such as the Numayrids. From 2014 to 2017 it was the de facto capital of the Islamic State group.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raqqa.jpg" alt="Raqqa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User Zeledi on en.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raqqa_2003-12-31.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Raqqa lies on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, about 160 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. The mounds of Tall Zaydan and Tall al-Bi'a, the second identified with the Babylonian city of Tuttul, show that the area was inhabited from remote antiquity. The modern city traces its history to the Hellenistic foundation of Nikephorion, which Pliny attributes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and Appian to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a>. Seleucus II Callinicus enlarged it and renamed it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kallinikos">Kallinikos</a> after himself. In Roman times it belonged to the province of Osrhoene.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Byzantine emperor Leo I rebuilt the city in 466, but the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kallinikos">Kallinikos</a> prevailed. By treaty it was one of the few official cross-border trading posts between the Byzantine and Sasanian empires. It fell to the Muslim commander Iyad ibn Ghanm in 639 or 640 and took the Arabic name al-Raqqah, 'the morass', after its marshy surroundings. Its Christians were allowed to keep their existing churches but not to build new ones. In 770-1 the Abbasid caliph al-Mansur began a new garrison city, al-Rafiqah, about 200 metres west of Raqqa, to strengthen the frontier with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>; about 2,660 metres of its walls survive, with its congregational mosque.</p><p>The caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> made the city his capital from 796. Raqqa declined in the late 9th century through warfare between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulunid-dynasty">Tulunids</a> and the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a>, and declined rapidly under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a> in the 940s. From the late 10th century to the early 12th it was controlled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> dynasties such as the Banu Numayr. Under the Zangid and Ayyubid dynasties it flourished again on agriculture and industry, and the blue-glazed Raqqa ware dates from then. The Mongol invasions of the 1260s destroyed it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> established a garrison there in 1864, which began the revival of the modern city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Raqqa is a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Its growth in the 1950s was stimulated by a worldwide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> boom, and cotton is still the main agricultural product of the region. The Baghdad Gate, the Qasr al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> and the Abbasid walls and mosque are among the surviving monuments. From 2014 to 2017 the city was the capital of the Islamic State. The Raqqa Museum held many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets, some excavated at Tell Sabi Abyad, which survive today as casts and 3D reconstructions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raqqa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1182 Kukbary of Harran defected and invited Saladin into the Jazira: its cities fell one by one, and on 12 June 1183 Zangi handed Aleppo to Saladin for Sinjar, Nusaybin and <b>Raqqa</b>, ending Zengid rule in Syria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Iberian War, a Sasanian Persian army defeated the Byzantine army of Belisarius at Callinicum (<b>Raqqa</b>), in northern Syria, on Easter Saturday.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-19">19 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The army of Caliph Harun al-Rashid, far larger than any assembled before, left <b>Raqqa</b>, crossed Cilicia and the Taurus Mountains and invaded Byzantine Cappadocia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-11">11 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 990, as Hamdanid power faded, their chief Waththab ibn Sabiq seized Harran, and the tribe went on to hold Saruj, <b>Raqqa</b> and for a time Edessa.</blockquote><a href="/state/numayrids">Numayrids</a></li></ul></section>
